; ======================================================================
;  hw_mod_q.unoq - THE UNO Q's HALF OF THE DRIVER-MODULE CONTRACT
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  REQUIRES, in this order, in the main file:
;      Anvil/Hal/seams.pbi              the #SVCCAP_* vocabulary
;      ArduinoQ/Board/qstubs_q.unoq     HwMonRegion* / HwPay*
;
;  THIS BOARD DECLARES NO MODULE ARENA YET, AND SAYS SO RATHER THAN
;  INVENTING ONE. That is the whole content of this file and it is a
;  deliberate answer, not a stub left behind.
;
;  WHY IT CANNOT BE A CONSTANT HERE THE WAY IT IS ON THE Pi 4. This
;  monitor is a UEFI application: the firmware chooses where the image
;  lands, owns every byte of DRAM it has not handed out, and publishes a
;  memory map that is not the same twice. There is no round megabyte in
;  this part's map that belongs to Anvil by construction - which is
;  exactly why HwMonLo() and HwMonHi() are procedures on this board and
;  constants on the other one.
;
;  WHAT THE REAL ANSWER IS, WRITTEN DOWN SO THE NEXT LANE DOES NOT HAVE
;  TO DERIVE IT:
;
;    AllocatePages(AllocateAnyPages, EfiLoaderData, n) through
;    ArduinoQ/Board/qefi_q.unoq's boot-services table. UEFI pages are
;    4 KiB, which is exactly the alignment the loader requires, and the
;    firmware's allocator is the one authority on this board about what
;    is free - so asking it is strictly better than any window a person
;    could choose.
;
;    ONE Q-ONLY RULE COMES WITH IT: pages taken from boot services must
;    be FREED when a module fails to activate, or Anvil leaks them across
;    a reset. The loader already unwinds a failed activation's services;
;    the pages are the board's to give back, so the free belongs in this
;    file beside the allocate, not in the core.
;
;    It is not written today because it cannot be tested today: there is
;    no UNO Q on this bench, and a firmware allocation that has never run
;    is not something to ship as though it had. Compilation is not
;    acceptance, and this file refusing honestly is a better state than
;    an untested allocator that reports success.
;
;  UNTIL THEN: HwModArenaBase() answers 0, ModArenaInit() refuses with
;  #MOD_ERR_NOARENA, the boot walk stays silent about it, and `mod` says
;  in one sentence that this board declares no arena. Everything else in
;  the loader - the container reader, the validator, the relocator, the
;  registry, the lifecycle and the console - compiles and links here
;  identically to the Pi 4, which is the build-closure claim this file
;  exists to keep true.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i HwModArenaBase()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwModArenaBytes()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; No region of this board's map is the arena, so there is no index for
; ModArenaInit() to skip. -1 matches no region.
Procedure.i HwModArenaRegion()
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwModStageAddr()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwModStageBytes()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwModCodeSync(base, bytes) - REFUSES, and refusing is the correct
;  answer rather than a placeholder.
;
;  The loader calls this after it has written a module's bytes and before
;  anything branches into them, and it treats a non-1 answer as
;  #MOD_ERR_SYNC: the module is NOT initialised and the arena bytes are
;  cleared again. Answering 1 without performing the maintenance would
;  make the loader believe a cache state nobody established, which is the
;  one failure in this whole path that produces no message at all - a
;  branch into stale instruction lines.
;
;  Anvil runs at EL1 under UEFI here with the firmware's caches and MMU
;  configuration, and this project has not established what maintenance
;  is permitted from that state on this part. Until it has, this returns
;  0 and nothing is ever executed from the arena - which is consistent
;  with there being no arena.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwModCodeSync(base.i, bytes.i)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE DEVICES. None are declared, and that is the truth rather than an
;  omission: nothing on this part has been enumerated to the point where
;  a driver could be handed a block and told it is safe to read. The
;  QCM2290 is the part where a speculative read of an unclocked block
;  cost a power cycle (RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_addr.pi4 records the
;  contrast), so a device list here is a list of things a probe is
;  allowed to touch, and it may not be guessed.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwDevCount()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwDevIdAddr(i.i)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwDevSeam(i.i)
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwDevToken(i.i)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure HwDevSay(i.i)
  Print("a device this board does not declare")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE CAPABILITY QUESTIONS. Both answer 0 for both new ids: this board
;  reaches neither seam from the core, and neither piece of hardware has
;  been established as present in a form a driver could use. A board that
;  claimed otherwise would send an operator looking for a module to load
;  that could never bind to anything.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwSeamCore(id.i)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwSeamPossible(id.i)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
